  "query_preview": "..The robust thunderstorm activity that occurred last \nnight, especially to the west to northwest of Tucson is winding down \nfor our area this morning with an MCV now near Phoenix. However, \nseparate from that we have been seeing some renewed isolated to \nscattered shower and embedded thunderstorm development in the past \nhour or so over central Pima County. This is out ahead of a weak \ndisturbance aloft across northern Sonora per latest WV imagery. \nExpect this activity to continue into the early morning hours, \nthough not be particularly impactful. \n\nThe bigger picture mid/upper level pattern this morning continues to \nbe defined by a broad east-west oriented high pressure aloft from \nNew Mexico into west Texas with southeasterly flow continuing for \nour area. Renewed shower and thunderstorm activity this afternoon \ninto this evening should remain limited as the region will be on the \nsubsident side of the aforementioned disturbance, though adequate \nmoisture and instability continues mainly from the Tucson area \nwestward to justify a slight chance (~10%) of showers and \nthunderstorms. Temperatures today will be a couple of degrees cooler \ncompared to yesterday but still running about 3 to 7 degrees above \nnormal. \n\nAn extended stretch of late season heat will be the main weather \nstory for the work week as the high pressure aloft strengthens and \nnudges west into Arizona by Wednesday and remains quasi-stationary \nthrough Friday. The drier air and subsidence aloft with the high \nwill result in diminished monsoonal thunderstorm activity the rest \nof the work week. While this won't completely shut down \nthunderstorms, it will solidly be low grade for much of the week, \npicking up somewhat as early as Friday but more so this weekend as \nthe high starts to shift a bit more eastward into a more favorable \nposition.  \n\nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday with high \ntemperatures expected to peak midweek 6 to 10 degrees above normal.",
